course: dictionary
lesson: 7692
title: Proudly: showing pride
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Proudly is an adverb. It tells us how someone does something, and it comes from the word proud. When a character does something proudly, they feel pleased about it. «Then he edits with the checklist and shares it proudly» tells you the boy is pleased with his work, so he wants to show it to others.
explain: Proudly can also mean showing you think you are better than other people. Read the clues around it to find out which meaning is right. «Alice has read about courts in books and proudly knows the judge by his wig» shows she is pleased to know these things, not that she is looking down on others.
explain: Proudly can also mean in a grand, impressive way, as when a castle is standing proudly on a hill. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Proudly means in a way that shows pride.
fact: Proudly can mean pleased about something you or someone close to you did, or showing you think you are better than others.
fact: Phrases like «smiled proudly», «proudly showed» and «stood proudly» tell the reader how a character feels or how something looks.
fact: Proudly is an adverb, so its comparative is more proudly and its superlative is most proudly.
fact: Arrogantly is a synonym for the show-off meaning of proudly, and it is much less friendly than simply being pleased.
exercise: «Then he edits with the checklist and shares it proudly.» What does proudly show? | He is angry about it | He is hiding it from everyone | He is tired of it | *He is pleased with his work
exercise: «The castle stood proudly on the hill.» Which meaning fits best? | In a quick, rushing way | In a quiet, hidden way | *In a grand, impressive way | In a sad, lonely way
exercise: «Alice proudly knows the judge by his wig.» Which feeling does proudly show? | Boredom with the court | Fear of the judge | Sadness about the trial | *Pride in what she already knows
test: Which word is a synonym for proudly when it means showing you think you are better than others? | *arrogantly | gently | quietly | sadly
test: What does the phrase «smiled proudly» tell the reader? | *The character feels pride | The character is lost | The character is running fast | The character feels cold
exam: You meet proudly in a text and are not sure what it means. What should you do first? | *Read the words around it and try your meaning in the sentence | Ignore the sentence and look only at the title | Skip the whole text and never come back to it | Guess the first meaning that comes to mind and stop reading
